# Where the DisjointMut tracker's time actually goes, and the one knob that moved it

Host: Apple M4 Pro (8P+4E), macOS 26.5.2, 12 cores.
Base: `fix/aarch64-mc-itx` @ 2c7c082 (PR #453) — verify/compose plus the
aarch64 correctness fixes, 766/766 dav1d-test-data.
Default features on every arm. No `nice` on any timed run. No
`-C target-cpu=native`.

The brief was "halve the tracker's guard traffic", with two named targets:
`WithOffset::block_mut`'s double reservation, and `CompInterType`'s guard drop
glue. Both were profiled. One is a documented negative and the other is a
mirage. The thing that actually moved is `BLOCK_SHIFT`.


## 1. Profile first — where the guard traffic is

`scripts/perf/prof_sample.sh`, `bench_base`, v4k_8tile 8bpc, t=8, 45 s.
259,480 leaf samples, of which 56,160 are `__psynch_cvwait` (idle waits), so
203,320 are work. Attribution is by nearest NON-tracker ancestor, so
registration and release are separated from the code that asked for them.

    caller                                   add     drop    total   % all   % work
    -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    compact_read_per_row (all callers)     12131    19763    31894   12.29   15.69
    LfBlock::open (loopfilter tap read)     8393    12881    21274    8.20   10.46
    drop_glue<BlockMut> (itx write-back)    3410     3561     6971    2.69    3.43
    decode_b                                 775     3335     4110    1.58    2.02
    ipred::splat_dc                         2806     1430     4236    1.63    2.08
    cdef_filter_block_8bpc_inner            1478     2474     3952    1.52    1.94
    ipred::cfl_pred                         2181     1751     3932    1.52    1.93
    angular_ipred                           1750     1701     3451    1.33    1.70
    rav1d_recon_b_intra                     1296     1519     2815    1.08    1.38
    rav1d_prepare_intra_edges (direct)       862     1922     2784    1.07    1.37
    -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    TOTAL tracker                          38851    53451    92302   35.58   45.40

`TinyLock::lock_slow` is 18,544 (7.15%) and lies INSIDE the add column:
9,624 under `add::<false>`, 4,848 under `add::<true>`.

Two corrections to the brief's reading of the code:

* **The `CompInterType` guard drop glue is not `CompInterType`.** The symbol
  `drop_glue<DisjointImmutGuard<Aligned<A8, [Option<CompInterType>; 32]>>>`
  carries 15.19% of leaf samples, which reads like `env.rs`'s block-context
  lookups. It is not. The drop glue is identical machine code for every guard
  whose payload needs no drop, so the linker folds them all onto whichever
  mangled name survives — and that happened to be this one. Its callers are
  `compact_read_per_row` (19,763) and `LfBlock::open` (12,881), not `env.rs`.
  Attributing by parent instead of by symbol is what unmasks it.

* **`compact_read_per_row`'s biggest caller is not `block_mut`.** Splitting it:

        rav1d_prepare_intra_edges   23837   9.19%   <- 1-PIXEL-WIDE COLUMN
        block_mut                   14081   5.43%

  `ipred_prepare.rs` reads the left edge and the bottom-left edge through
  `with_pixel_guard_immut(col_pic, /*w*/ 1, px_have)`, which under tile
  threading becomes `compact_read_per_row(1, h)` — **one tracker registration
  per row to read ONE BYTE per row**, up to `4 * th` = 64 of them per edge, for
  every intra block. That is the worst guards-per-byte ratio in the decoder and
  it is 9.19% of a 4K frame on its own.


## 2. The mechanism: it is the shard cache line, not the atomics

`remove` was `swap`-acquire + bit clear + `store`-release. Making the occupancy
bitmap atomic and dropping the lock takes it to one `fetch_and` — a third of
the line traffic gone from a path the profile blamed for 20.6% of samples.

Measured: **-0.9%** (119.4 vs 120.5 ms/frame, v4k_8tile 8bpc t=8, interleaved
median of 5). Essentially nothing.

That is the finding. A strided access registers one interval per ROW; at a
3840-byte row and a 4096-byte block no two rows share a block, so `shard_of`
sends each row to a different one of the instance's 128 shard lines. What the
profile calls "guard drop cost" is the core waiting for a line, and removing
atomic operations from it does not remove the wait. Fewer LINES does.


## 3. The BLOCK_SHIFT ladder

`examples/probe_tracker` (`--features probe-wide`), v4k_8tile, t=8, 6 iters,
in-process timer, one foreign core busy. Every arm built with its own
`cargo build --features` invocation and sha256-verified distinct.

    shift   bytes/block  rows/block   8bpc ms/f   10bpc ms/f   add_slow(8b)
    ------------------------------------------------------------------------
       12          4096        1.07       119.0        140.7          72585
       13          8192        2.13        89.3        130.1          36197
       14         16384        4.27        72.8        100.4          18061
       15         32768        8.53        73.0         92.1           9038
       16         65536       17.07        75.5         91.0           4454

`rows/block` is at the 8-bit luma stride of 3840. `add_slow` == `add_multi`
here (every entry was a multi-block borrow) and halves with each step, exactly
as a doubling block size predicts.

**Wide-path promotions: ZERO at every shift, on both bit depths.**
`WIDE_SHARDS` / `WIDE_BLOCKS` / `WIDE_FULL` all read 0 for 12, 13, 14, 15 and
16. The knob does not push anything onto the all-shards path.


## 4. `blockshift-adaptive`: the shift as a function of the buffer, not a constant

A global constant cannot be right at every size: the shift that makes a 4K
plane's rows share a block turns a 64 KiB buffer into ONE block and one lock.
`block_shift_for(len)` picks the power of two that lands `len` on about
`BLOCKS_PER_SHARD * N_SHARDS` blocks — at 128 shards and a ratio of 2 that is
`log2(len) - 8`, clamped to [6, 24].

Because a 10-bit plane is twice the bytes AND twice the stride of its 8-bit
twin, a rule keyed on `len` alone tracks the stride for free:

    buffer                        len        shift   blocks   rows/block
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    64 KiB (SHARD_MIN_LEN)      65536            8      256            -
    1024x1024 8-bit           1048576           12      256          4.0
    4K chroma 4:2:0 8-bit     2073600           13      253          4.3
    4K luma 8-bit             8294400           14      506          4.3
    4K luma 10-bit           16588800           15      506          4.3
    8x over-allocation       66355200           17      506          4.3

Pinned by `adaptive_shift_keeps_the_block_count_near_target`.


## 5. THE SHIPPED RESULT — idle box, median of 5, zero foreign

`scripts/perf/verify_gap.sh`, arms `base` (2c7c082) vs `newdef` (the shipped
default: adaptive shift once parallelism is declared). Wall clock of the whole
process at 2 and 20 frames, `total = alpha + beta*frames` fitted so startup
drops out. Both arms run back to back per cell, order rotated per round, every
cell re-run until it commits with ZERO foreign processes over 25% CPU. Raw:
`benchmarks/tracker_blockshift_confirm_2026-08-08.tsv`.

    vector             t     base   newdef   ratio
    ------------------------------------------------
    v4k_8tile          4    131.3    119.8    0.91
    v4k_8tile          8    117.9     76.3    0.65
    v4k_8tile_10b      4    162.0    155.3    0.96
    v4k_8tile_10b      8    141.6     97.1    0.69
    v1024              8     27.2     26.9    0.99
    v4k_1tile          8    364.6    373.9    1.03   <- COST

Against dav1d 1.5.4 `--framedelay 1` on this host with this harness
(`benchmarks/verify_compose_2026-08-07.meta`: 246.5 / 125.2 / 65.6 / 37.4 at
t=1/2/4/8 8bpc, and 10bpc derivable from its ratio row as 251 / 127 / 66.8 /
37.9):

    v4k_8tile 8bpc     t=4  2.00x -> 1.83x     t=8  3.15x -> 2.04x
    v4k_8tile 10bpc    t=4  2.43x -> 2.32x     t=8  3.74x -> 2.56x

**The cost is real and reproducible: single-tile 4K at t=8 is 2.6% slower**
across all five rounds. One tile means the recon task is serial and the
concurrency is post-filter tasks sharing the same planes, so the coarser block
buys no strided-read locality and only adds collisions. It is not detectable
from the buffer length, which is all the tracker knows, so it is not gated out.
1024x1024 is neutral (0.99).


## 5b. Earlier, thinner table — all five arms, n=1

Superseded by 5 for the ship decision, kept because it is the only measurement
that separates `lfrem` / `bs14` / `bs16` / `adapt` from each other on an idle
box.

Wall clock of the whole process at two frame counts, `total = alpha + beta*frames`
fitted so startup drops out; `beta` is ms/frame. Every arm run back to back per
cell with the order rotated per round; a cell is DISCARDED and re-run whenever
any foreign process exceeds 25% CPU. Raw: `benchmarks/tracker_blockshift_gap_2026-08-08.tsv`.

    vector             t     base    lfrem     bs14     bs16    adapt
    -----------------------------------------------------------------
    v4k_8tile          1    404.0    408.2    387.4    384.9    384.6
    v4k_8tile          2    223.1    223.2    221.2    222.2    221.3
    v4k_8tile          4    156.1    134.0    122.3    126.4    126.5
    v4k_8tile          8    119.9    116.7     77.0     80.0     78.9
    v4k_8tile_10b      1    510.9    507.6    518.6    509.2    528.9
    v4k_8tile_10b      2    274.6    274.2    273.3    272.8    276.9
    v4k_8tile_10b      4    183.5    159.9    154.1    154.3    154.1
    v4k_8tile_10b      8    139.2    139.5    107.6     97.2     98.3

**n = 1 round for every cell except v4k_8tile t=1 (n = 2).** The box carried
two agents' perf campaigns for most of this session and idle windows were
short; the effect at t=4/t=8 is 5-10x the cell-to-cell spread, the t=1 and
t=2 columns are not resolvable at this n. The loaded-box interleaved screens
(median of 5-6, both arms seeing the same load) agree on the t=8 figure to
within 2 points.

`adapt` here is the ALWAYS-ON arm (`--features blockshift-adaptive`). The
shipped default only adapts once `set_parallelism` has been told there is
more than one thread, so its t=1 rows are `base`'s by construction — which is
also why the one adverse cell in the table, v4k_8tile_10b t=1 at +3.5%,
cannot reach a shipped configuration.

dav1d 1.5.4 `--framedelay 1` on this host and these vectors, same harness and
same wall fit, is 246.5 / 125.2 / 65.6 / 37.4 at t=1/2/4/8 8bpc
(`benchmarks/verify_compose_2026-08-07.meta`); this campaign's own dav1d arm
was pointed at the wrong vector directory and then never got an idle window,
so the ratios below are computed against THAT measurement, not one taken
alongside these rows:

    v4k_8tile 8bpc   t=1 1.56x   t=2 1.77x   t=4 1.93x   t=8 2.11x
    (was, at base)       1.64x       1.78x       2.38x       3.21x


## 6. Soundness of the shift, mutation-proven

The adaptive shift rests on exactly one premise: **both registrants of a shared
byte agree on where the block boundary is.** They do, because the shift is read
once in `BorrowTracker::new` and immutable for the tracker's life (it moves only
in `reprovision`, which takes `&mut self`).

Planting the negation — `let shift = self.block_shift() + (COUNTER.fetch_add(1)
& 1)`, so consecutive registrations disagree by one bit — makes
`cross_shard_overlaps_are_all_caught` FAIL, and restoring it makes 22/22 pass
again. That is the test that sweeps 64 block offsets looking for an overlap the
sharding lets slip, and it is the one that has to fail if the premise is
violated.

Two more mutation proofs cover the lock-free release:

* `publish` written as `occupied.store(occ | bit)` from the lock holder's
  snapshot — the lost-update shape — makes `threaded_churn_leaks_no_slots` fail
  in under 50k iterations with a spurious `overlapping DisjointMut` against a
  record whose release was dropped.
* `find` fed a hardcoded empty occupancy — detection switched off — fails
  `same_block_overlap_is_caught`, `cross_shard_overlaps_are_all_caught`,
  `immutable_sharing_then_mutable_conflict` and `test_overlapping_mut`.


## 7. Measured negatives — do not re-run these

**`held-row-guards` (the brief's named `block_mut` target): NULL.**
Taking the per-row MUTABLE guards up front and holding them across the kernel
does halve `block_mut`'s registrations from `2h` to `h`, the extents are
byte-identical, and it is 766/766 correct. It measures 131.3 vs 128.5 ms/frame
at shift 12, t=8 — inside the noise and if anything worse. Halving the
registration COUNT does not help when the cost is the line, because holding
`h` guards touches the same `h` lines the two-pass shape did. Kept behind a
feature, default off.

**`held-row-guards` + a larger block: CATASTROPHIC, and it is the only thing
that ever reached the wide path.** shift 15 + held guards measured 402 ms/frame
against a 120 ms base; shift 16 + held guards + `shard-ident` measured 986 ms.
Profile of the 402 ms arm: `TinyLock::lock_slow` 55.18%, `add_wide::<true>`
7.58%, `remove_wide` 4.50%. Mechanism: holding up to 64 row guards concentrates
records on few shards once a block spans several rows; a shard holds `SLOTS`=7
before promoting, and `add_wide` locks EVERY active shard of the instance. The
two features are individually harmless and jointly a collapse — which is the
reason the held-guards experiment stays off rather than merely unmeasured.

**`shard-ident` (identity instead of Fibonacci shard mapping): -12.4% at shift
12 alone** (105.5 vs 120.5), and superseded — it is the same locality effect
the shift gets more of. Not re-measured on top of the shift; kept as a knob.

**A TinyLock backoff arm was already measured null twice** by an earlier
campaign and was not re-run. Nothing in this profile suggests otherwise:
`lock_slow` is 7.15% at shift 12 and falls with the shift.


## 8. Traps this campaign walked into — so nobody repeats them

1. **Two agents' perf sweeps on one box.** The first ladder screen ran while
   another worktree's 8-thread `verify_gap.sh` held 763% CPU. Absolute numbers
   were inflated ~1.6x and one arm's ordering was inverted. `verify_gap.sh`'s
   idle guard protects the arm it is running, not the agent who forgets to look
   at `ps` first. Check `pgrep -f verify_gap` before launching, and check
   `.workongoing` in the sibling worktrees.

2. **zsh does not word-split unquoted `${1:+--features "$1"}`.** A build helper
   using that idiom passed cargo a single argument `--features blockshift-14`,
   and every arm in the loop was staged from one stale binary. Eight "different"
   arms measured identical to 0.5%, which reads exactly like a null result.
   **sha256 the staged binaries and assert they are distinct** before believing
   any A/B.

3. **A hot global counter destroys what it measures.** A `fetch_add` on one
   shared `AtomicU64` per registration is 136 M RMWs per 4K frame from eight
   threads on one cache line. It serialised the decoder hard enough that shard
   slot pressure disappeared and `WIDE_FULL` read zero for the wrong reason.
   The wide-path counters now only count events that fire 10^4 times per frame,
   and `N_ADD` is deliberately not incremented.

4. **`__probe_count` switches the crate to the LEGACY tracker.** Counters added
   to `tracker_shard.rs` are dead code under it and silently read zero. That is
   why `__probe_wide` exists as a separate feature that keeps the sharded one.

5. **Symbol self-time lies when drop glue is folded.** See section 1.
